    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p19-q16a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If $a$ and $b$ are both irrational, then does this imply that $a+b$ is irrational? Give a proof or counterexample.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No. For example, $a=\\sqrt2$ and $b=-\\sqrt2$ are irrational, but $a+b=0$ is rational.",
          "worked": "Take $a=\\sqrt2$ and $b=-\\sqrt2$. Both numbers are irrational, but\n$$a+b=\\sqrt2+(-\\sqrt2)=0,$$\nwhich is rational. This counterexample disproves the implication.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q16b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If $a$ is irrational and $b$ is rational, then does this imply that $a+b$ is irrational? Give a proof or counterexample.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Yes. If $a+b$ were rational, then $a=(a+b)-b$ would be rational, a contradiction.",
          "worked": "Suppose $a+b$ is rational. Since $b$ is rational,\n$$a=(a+b)-b$$\nwould be rational, because the difference of two rational numbers is rational. This contradicts that $a$ is irrational. Therefore $a+b$ is irrational.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
  },
